{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fcv56MnVLcc2PGPEdfzkCJ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `picoquic/picosocks.h` plus the Phase 2 socket\n2\t//! abstraction.\n3\t//!\n4\t//! Phase 2 reshape: the C `SOCKET_TYPE` (a Linux file descriptor)\n5\t//! is replaced by a [`Socket`] *trait* that the host platform\n6\t//! implements.  The default implementation wraps\n7\t//! [`socket2::Socket`] (the canonical cross-platform UDP socket\n8\t//! crate); other backends (no-std embedded, Windows-specific,\n9\t//! the test simulator) implement the same trait.\n10\t//! [`ServerSockets<S>`] is generic over the implementor.\n11\t//!\n12\t//! Phase 4: all method bodies are implemented.\n13\t\n14\tuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n15\t\n16\tuse crate::Error;\n17\tuse crate::Instant;\n18\t\n19\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n20\t// Constants and supporting types.\n21\t\n22\t/// Number of UDP sockets a server context owns — one IPv4, one\n23\t/// IPv6.  C: `PICOQUIC_NB_SERVER_SOCKETS`.\n24\tpub const SERVER_SOCKET_COUNT: usize = 2;\n25\t\n26\t/// `MessageHeader` is a re-export of [`socket2::MsgHdr`] — the\n27\t/// canonical cross-platform `msghdr` wrapper.  Phase 4 fills in\n28\t/// the bodies that thread one through the cmsg helpers.\n29\tpub type MessageHeader<'addr, 'bufs, 'control> = socket2::MsgHdr<'addr, 'bufs, 'control>;\n30\t\n31\t/// OS error code surfaced by socket sends when the underlying\n32\t/// syscall fails.  Wraps the raw Linux `errno` (or its Windows\n33\t/// equivalent on other targets) and exposes\n34\t/// [`OsError::is_unreachable`] for the path-abandon decision the\n35\t/// C `picoquic_socket_error_implies_unreachable` helper made.\n36\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n37\tpub struct OsError(pub i32);\n38\t\n39\timpl OsError {\n40\t    /// Whether this errno implies the destination is unreachable\n41\t    /// and the owning path should be abandoned.  C:\n42\t    /// `picoquic_socket_error_implies_unreachable`.\n43\t    pub fn is_unreachable(self) -> bool {\n44\t        use std::io::ErrorKind;\n45\t        let kind = std::io::Error::from_raw_os_error(self.0).kind();\n46\t        matches!(\n47\t            kind,\n48\t            ErrorKind::ConnectionReset\n49\t                | ErrorKind::HostUnreachable\n50\t                | ErrorKind::NetworkDown\n51\t                | ErrorKind::NetworkUnreachable\n52\t        )\n53\t    }\n54\t}\n55\t\n56\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n57\t// `Socket` trait.\n58\t\n59\t/// One UDP socket on the application's network stack.  Replaces\n60\t/// the C `SOCKET_TYPE` (a raw file descriptor); the host\n61\t/// implementation supplies open / bind / send / recv operations\n62\t/// while the rest of picoquic stays platform-agnostic.\n63\t///\n64\t/// The default [`socket2::Socket`] implementation is in\n65\t/// [`crate::socks_socket2`] (gated on the `std` feature).\n66\tpub trait Socket {\n67\t    /// Local address bound to this socket (`getsockname`).\n68\t    /// C: `picoquic_get_local_address`.\n69\t    fn local_address(&self) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error>;\n70\t\n71\t    /// Receive one UDP datagram into `buffer`.  Output parameters\n72\t    /// (source / dest address, interface index, ECN, byte count)\n73\t    /// fold into [`RecvInfo`]; the slice length subsumes the C\n74\t    /// `buffer_max` parameter.  C: `picoquic_recvmsg`.\n75\t    fn recv(&mut self, buffer: &mut [u8]) -> Result<RecvInfo, Error>;\n76\t\n77\t    /// Send `bytes` to `addr_dest` from `addr_from` with an\n78\t    /// optional GSO segmentation hint `gso_size` (`UDP_SEGMENT`).\n79\t    /// `addr_from = None` lets the kernel choose the source\n80\t    /// address.  `Ok(n)` reports bytes sent; `Err` carries the\n81\t    /// OS error.  C: `picoquic_sendmsg`.\n82\t    fn send(\n83\t        &mut self,\n84\t        addr_dest: &SocketAddr,\n85\t        addr_from: Option<&SocketAddr>,\n86\t        dest_if: i32,\n87\t        bytes: &[u8],\n88\t        gso_size: i32,\n89\t    ) -> Result<usize, OsError>;\n90\t\n91\t    /// Enable per-packet destination-info delivery on this\n92\t    /// socket (`IP_PKTINFO` / `IPV6_RECVPKTINFO`).  Default:\n93\t    /// no-op for backends that don't surface pktinfo.\n94\t    fn set_pkt_info(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n95\t        Ok(())\n96\t    }\n97\t\n98\t    /// Enable ECN reception (and request `ECN_ECT_1` outbound).\n99\t    /// Returns `(recv_set, send_set)` indicating whether the\n100\t    /// kernel honored each socket option.  Default: no-op,"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ce9760c1-0503-4891-9073-2bbfbb3810ec","uuid":"4b058755-e5c4-4e38-99c3-85a01533f4b1","timestamp":"2026-05-07T07:43:55.583Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/socks.rs","content":"//! Translation of `picoquic/picosocks.h` plus the Phase 2 socket\n//! abstraction.\n//!\n//! Phase 2 reshape: the C `SOCKET_TYPE` (a Linux file descriptor)\n//! is replaced by a [`Socket`] *trait* that the host platform\n//! implements.  The default implementation wraps\n//! [`socket2::Socket`] (the canonical cross-platform UDP socket\n//! crate); other backends (no-std embedded, Windows-specific,\n//! the test simulator) implement the same trait.\n//! [`ServerSockets<S>`] is generic over the implementor.\n//!\n//! Phase 4: all method bodies are implemented.\n\nuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n\nuse crate::Error;\nuse crate::Instant;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Constants and supporting types.\n\n/// Number of UDP sockets a server context owns — one IPv4, one\n/// IPv6.  C: `PICOQUIC_NB_SERVER_SOCKETS`.\npub const SERVER_SOCKET_COUNT: usize = 2;\n\n/// `MessageHeader` is a re-export of [`socket2::MsgHdr`] — the\n/// canonical cross-platform `msghdr` wrapper.  Phase 4 fills in\n/// the bodies that thread one through the